# Append-only ledgers: union merge auto-concatenates both sides on conflict.
# EVERY ledger bkit workflows append to is listed — one left out blocks a merge
# over data that never conflicted, two branches having simply appended different
# rows. JSONL rows dedupe by identity tuple (kind, repo, key); union may interleave
# or duplicate lines, resolved by the reader — never a blocking merge conflict.
#
# Union makes append-only LOAD-BEARING, not a convention. It keeps both sides of a
# conflicting hunk, so editing a row in place leaves the stale copy alive beside its
# replacement — and the reader folds last-wins by `ts`, so the stale row wins
# whenever its `ts` is the later one. Correct a row by APPENDING one with the same
# `key` and a CURRENT `ts`; never edit or delete a line, and never write a future
# `ts` (it outranks every correction that follows it, permanently).
docs/knowledge.jsonl merge=union
docs/decisions.jsonl merge=union
docs/deferrals.jsonl merge=union
docs/failures.jsonl  merge=union
.bkit/issues.jsonl   merge=union
docs/roadmap.md      merge=union
.bkit/stats.jsonl    merge=union

# `.bkit/friction.jsonl` is deliberately absent: it is the one ledger rows are
# REMOVED from (bkit's harvest drains it). Union would resurrect rows a drain
# deleted, so a conflict there carries real information and must stay blocking.

# Append-only NDJSON workflow status logs: union auto-concatenates both branches'
# appended lines server-side so a per-FID status log never blocks a merge.
# Safe iff strictly append-only (prior lines never mutated); the reader folds + dedupes
# by (workflow, step, parentSha, at) and orders by `at`, tolerating interleave/duplication.
# Scoped to bkit's status files only — NOT a blanket *.jsonl (union would corrupt a
# consumer's non-append-only .jsonl data on merge).
docs/plans/**/status.jsonl merge=union

# <!-- bkit:end -->

# Local opt-in, against the managed block's advice above (Tier-2 bkit-repo rule).
# `.bkit/friction.jsonl` IS drained by `pnpm harvest`, so union can resurrect rows a
# drain deleted. Accepted here: this repo runs the workflows that append friction on
# every cycle, drains are rare and deliberate, and a resurrected row is re-banked
# idempotently by the next harvest — cheaper than a blocking conflict per cycle.
# A consumer with the opposite balance should leave it unset and resolve by hand.
.bkit/friction.jsonl  merge=union
